  "reconstructibility": 1,
  "reason": "The records collectively point toward temperature-dependent mechanical degradation and deformation-mechanism changes in closely related FCC CoCrFeMnNi-family HEAs. In particular, R1 and R11 connect dislocation structures to temperature-dependent strength, R15 connects increasing temperature to suppression of stacking faults, and R18 supplies molecular-dynamics evidence for temperature-dependent twinning and FCC-HCP transformations. R5 also establishes elevated-temperature friction and wear testing of an HEA film. However, none combines a FeNiCrCoCu nanoscale coating on Cu with finite-temperature nanoindentation and friction simulations, nor do the records specifically predict the sharp disordering, dislocation-density reduction, and friction increase above 900 K. Thus, they provide a plausible general direction, but selecting the specific coated system, simulation protocol, and high-temperature lattice-disorder mechanism would require a substantial new idea."
